## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical knowledge of testing streaming systems, with emphasis on edge-case window behavior, fault tolerance, and end-to-end accuracy—skills essential for building production-grade data platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Windowing semantics and latency boundaries
- Idempotent and deduplicated alerting
- End-to-end streaming integration patterns
- Backpressure handling and burst tolerance
- Observability and feature drift detection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unit test for window aggregation correctness
async function testWindowAggregation() {
  const windowService = new WindowingService({
    windowSizeMs: 5000,
    slideIntervalMs: 1000
  });

  // Simulate late-arriving events within watermark tolerance
  await windowService.processEvents([
    { timestamp: 1000, value: 10 },
    { timestamp: 2000, value: 20 },
    { timestamp: 5500, value: 30 } // Late event within tolerance
  ]);

  const result = await windowService.getWindowResult();
  expect(result.sum).toBe(60);
  expect(result.count).toBe(3);
}

// Integration test for alert deduplication
async function testAlertDeduplication() {
  const alertService = new AlertService();

  // Simulate duplicate events from Kafka retry
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    await alertService.process({
      eventId: 'evt-123',
      anomalyScore: 0.95,
      timestamp: Date.now()
    });
  }

  const alerts = await alertService.getEmittedAlerts();
  expect(alerts.length).toBe(1); // Only one alert emitted
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question explores a critical real-world scenario: testing serverless, event-driven processing with strict data integrity guarantees and partial-failure resilience. Candidates must demonstrate understanding of idempotency patterns, event-driven architectures, and comprehensive testing strategies.

## Key Concepts

- **Idempotency**: Ensuring duplicate S3 events for the same file produce the same result without duplication in DynamoDB
- **S3/Lambda Integration**: Understanding event-driven triggers and their testing challenges
- **Chunked Processing**: Handling large files efficiently without memory exhaustion
- **Partial Failure Resilience**: Maintaining accurate counts and data consistency when failures occur mid-processing
- **CI/CD Visibility**: Using mocks, stubs, and integration tests to verify correctness in automated pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Idempotent guard: check if file was already processed
async function handleEvent(event, db, s3) {
  const bucket = event.bucket;
  const key = event.key;

  const readStream = await s3.getObject(bucket, key).createReadStream();
  const checksum = await computeChecksum(readStream);

  const existing = await db.get(`FILE#${checksum}`);
  if (existing) {
    return { statusCode: 200, body: 'Already processed' };
  }

  await processChunks(readStream, db, checksum);
  await db.put(`FILE#${checksum}`, { processedAt: Date.now() });
}
```
